<Fragment slot="heading">The philosophy degree I didn’t know I <span style="font-style:italic">needed</span></Fragment>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Philosophy is probably the most overlooked degree in technology, and for years I overlooked mine too. I studied it about a decade before I moved into tech, when product management wasn’t on my radar, and I filed it under things I’d done before my real career started. Then I went back to study interactive design, app and game design, and the course coordinator pointed out that two of us in the class had philosophy backgrounds. He said it would be highly valuable in the work ahead. I hadn’t thought of it that way before.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The reputation is understandable. Nobody hires a product manager for their views on Kant. What the degree leaves you with is a habit: finding the assumption a claim is resting on, and arguing the other side properly before you reject it.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">Where it shows up</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Most product arguments are quietly arguments about words. Two teams disagree about whether something is a priority, or whether a feature is ‘done’, and each means something different by it. Asking what we mean by that sounds pedantic and usually saves a week. Prioritising has the same shape. You’re rarely choosing between a good idea and a bad one. You’re choosing between two good ones, and the team you turn down had a real reason to ask. I try to put their case well enough that they’d recognise it as theirs before I explain why we’re going the other way. If I can’t, I haven’t understood the decision yet.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-family:Newsreader,serif;font-size:25px;line-height:1.25;max-width:52ch">"If I can’t make the case for the thing I’m saying no to, I’m not ready to say no."</div>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">Why AI raises the stakes</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Conventional software did what someone had specified. AI systems act on judgement, so somebody has to decide what good judgement looks like. When should an assistant refuse, and whose interests win when the user and the business want different things? A model can’t settle that for you, and neither can a metric, because someone still had to decide what the metric should reward. I’d bet most teams file these under edge cases to tidy up later. I think they’re most of the design work, and they’re questions philosophy has been working on for a very long time.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">The nugget</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Next time two people are arguing past each other, ask each of them what they mean by the word doing the most work in their argument, usually something like ‘safe’ or ‘done’. Ten minutes of that tends to beat another hour of debate. And if there’s a philosophy graduate on your team, ask them. They’ve had years of practice and may never have thought to mention it.</p>
